The Nature of Vortex Ring State
Albert Brand∗ Mark Dreier Ron Kisor Tom Wood
Staff Engineer, Flight Technology Staff Engineer, Advanced Concepts Staff Engineer Director, Flight Technology and Preliminary Design V-22 Test and Technology IPT and Simulation Bell Helicopter Textron, Inc., Fort Worth, TX
At low forward speed, when a helicopter’s descent rate approaches the downward speed of its wake, the blade tip vortices organize in recirculating patterns beneath the rotor. A stable, ring-like wake structure emerges. Vortex ring state (VRS) is detected when the toroidal wake formation enters a buildup phase where organized vorticity accumulates more rapidly than it dissipates. The highly organized VRS wake dominates the descending rotor’s inflow field, reducing blade angle of attack and creating thrust loss. The main event, observed in piloted rotorcraft during VRS, occurs when the ring makes a single transition through the rotor plane from below. Steady speed wind tunnel tests do not appropriately simulate VRS because thrust loss does not lead to higher descent rates in a wind tunnel. A vortex ring can only propel itself in a single direction. Thus, prior to transition, a curved flight trajectory, or maneuver, will tend to preclude or suppress VRS.
Dominance of the Vortex Wake
References 1 and 2 describe a multiphase effort that dedicated over 100 flight test hours to the piloted exploration of vortex ring state (VRS). From the onset, it was apparent that wind tunnel testing had not fully explained many of the observations found in flight test. Numerous discussions led to the development of a flight test perspective on the nature of VRS. On this basis, a model supporting test observations was developed and is put forward. At its heart, VRS is primarily concerned with the rotor blade tip vortices and how they influence the rotor in a low forward airspeed descent. VRS investigation, therefore, must focus on understanding the nature of rotor blade tip vortices. While normally invisible, tip vortices are revealed on certain humid days, as seen for hovering and low-speed forward flight in Fig. 1. Even under ideal conditions, it is quite difficult to visualize tip vortices that are more than a few rotor revolutions old. The peak internal velocities subside as the vortex core ages. Eventually, the core’s low internal pressure begins to recover, visible moisture returns to the flow, and the helical pattern disappears. This observed dissipation in the near wake should not be interpreted as a reduction in vortex strength. When a vortex core dissipates, practically all the circulation it contains remains in the flow, just distributed over a larger region. Thus, the vortex continues to influence the rotor in accordance with its distributed strength, orientation, and proximity to the blades. The persistence of tip vortices near the rotor has always been the primary challenge to rotary wing flight and is a factor that fixed wing aerodynamicists may not fully appreciate. To illustrate, the idealized
