The combined helical tip vortex filaments have a powerful effect on the flow. The influence can be put into perspective by considering that a segment of the helical tip vortex (of length equal to the rotor radius) influences the local flow to the same extent as the bound circulation of an entire rotor blade. Yet, while there are only a few blades on any given rotor, there are hundreds of equivalent “blade lengths” of tip vortex filaments comprising the helical wake. The perhaps striking conclusion is that rotor blades do not produce significant airflow. Rotors and fans produce tip vortices, and these vortices are responsible for the flow sensed downstream of the rotor disk. This is why there is so little wind on the upstream side of a fan. This last observation is not intuitive from the principles of simple momentum theory and leads to the conclusion that the well-defined slipstream depicted for the hovering rotor cannot appear at all like the usual illustration of Fig. 2. The flow above the rotor is not bounded by a slipstream, as there is no mechanism for producing a defined shear layer where there are no vortices. Consequently, streamlines on the intake side of a hovering rotor approach the rotor disk from all directions, including below the rotor plane (as supported by experimental measurements in Ref. 3 and elsewhere). The true flow pattern, however, makes the derivation of simple momentum theory a challenge, hence, the “well-defined slipstream” extending far above the rotor disk. The discrepancy between simple momentum theory’s slipstream boundary and the actual flow near a rotor leads to more serious issues in the treatment of VRS. Simple momentum theory remains a surprisingly
useful tool for assessing the hover, climb, and windmilling performance of an idealized rotor. Yet, its inability to describe flow boundaries hints at its primary weakness, which is that it cannot establish the paramount role of wake vorticity in governing the rotor performance. As Fig. 2 illustrates, every rotor designed for vertical flight must operate in a flow that is dominated by the vortex wake. This observation is the key for developing a rational understanding of VRS. The existence of the wake creates a significant hover power requirement that is captured by the inflow of the idealized rotor in simple momentum theory. This “nonnegotiable” penalty means that improvements in rotor blade airfoil design have a comparatively small effect on increasing the overall hover performance of rotors. In a very general sense, wake vorticity never benefits the rotor, and when it is highly organized and concentrated, the performance penalty is significant. Figure 2 shows that the vortex-induced inflow requires the rotor blades to overcome a downwash field (vi), just to maintain hovering flight. In this respect, the inflow is equivalent to an effective climb rate imposed on a rotor that is, in reality, just maintaining its vertical position. The downwash penalty is illustrated in Fig. 3, which is the familiar velocity diagram representing the rotor blade airfoil in hover. It is seen that the resultant velocity at any blade element is the vector sum of its rotational speed ( r) and the wake-induced velocity, vi. The substantial result is that the hovering rotor blade airfoil is forced to operate at an effective angle of attack that is considerably lower than its geometric pitch angle. The point should be underscored, because the airfoil lift is proportional only to the effective angle of attack. This observation forms the basis of Prandtl’s hypothesis that allows aerodynamicists to estimate the lift of a three-dimensional finite wing using two-dimensional airfoil data. While l