The Dynamics of Descent
To fully appreciate the rotor wake in a descent, it is important to make a few observations regarding the behavior of vortices that are in close proximity to one another. The classical depiction of the rotor wake as a smoothly contracting cylindrical column is a reference point for many readers. However, because of local interactions, the vortex wake does not actually retain a smooth trajectory for its helical filaments. Within a few revolutions, the vortices interact with each other, merging and corotating as discussed in Ref. 3. In a climb, the geometric distortions can be greatly diminished because the filaments are spaced further apart due to the higher inflow provided by a climb rate. The greater spacing means that the influence of vortices on each other is less, and therefore, any distortions will take more time to develop. Conversely, when a rotor starts to descend, the downward-traveling wake competes with the vertical velocity of the rotor, Vz (positive for climb, negative for descent). In the helicopter reference frame, the descent velocity would appear to reduce the net inflow (vi + Vz). However, the vertical spacing between helical layers of the tip vortex filaments will be compressed. This compression not only increases the influence of the wake on the rotor (represented by the quantity vi), but also causes the helical wake pattern to begin distorting closer to the rotor. The initial wake distortion does not necessarily impact the rotor performance because the same amount of vorticity is still in the flow, and the influence on the rotor (in an integrated sense) is not substantially different from the idealized vortex wake geometry. However, the bundling of filaments has the effect of locally expanding the rotor wake. Later, in the modeling section of this paper, the wake is replaced by a series of simple vortex rings. It will be shown that in a descent, wake expansion promotes the reversal of the wake trajectory (toward the rotor) and the initiation of VRS. For low descent rates, the wake pattern close to the rotor remains intact, and wake bundling occurs farther downstream, as illustrated in Fig. 4. When a descent is initiated, compression of the wake starts with
