er (operating at a fixed 1g thrust level), a reduction in rotor mast torque is the obvious effect of achieving a steady low descent rate. As the descent rate continues to increase, additional compression takes place between successive layers of the helical wake. As before, this compression places more wake vorticity close to the rotor and tends to further increase inflow. However, the fundamental dynamic of VRS involves the tendency of closely spaced vortices to completely reshape the cylindrical wake and form a stable system of corotating/merging wake elements. The initiation of this behavior is depicted in Fig. 6, which shows the formation of recirculating vortices contained roughly within a toroidal structure beneath the descending rotor. Individual vortices located below the formation are forced to expand outward by the prevailing flow induced by the circulation of the torus. Any expanded wake element is itself like a vortex ring with a larger diameter. Diameter growth reduces the ability of that vortex ring element to propel itself downward (conservation of vorticity). This effect will become clearer when the “ring emitter” wake model is discussed later,
