Simple Ring Emitter Model in Pure Vertical Flight
In the transition to VRS, simple momentum theory breaks down because the observed wake geometry violates the “well-defined slipstream” requirement for analyzing flow states. Nevertheless, because momentum theory molds our understanding of rotor behavior, misconceptions regarding VRS abound. These misconceptions include notions that there is no flow through the rotor disk in VRS or that blade stall is responsible for the thrust loss observed in VRS measurements. The effect of VRS on a rotor blade element (as supported by Fig. 7) is illustrated in Fig. 8. For a fixed collective pitch, developing VRS will reduce the blade’s effective angle of attack because, in this regime, the induced velocity due to the accumulating organized vortex-ring wake grows faster than the opposing descent rate. As the net rotor inflow increases, the effective angle of attack of the blade decreases progressively. Rather than stalling, which would mean high angles of attack, the rotor in VRS loses thrust because of a reduction in the blade’s effective angle of attack that comes with the high inflow produced by VRS. The fact that inflow increases is a testament to the high level of wake organization during VRS and should serve to counter any claims arguing that wake disorganization is a contributor to thrust loss in VRS. As the organized wake column in hover produces the hover-induced velocity, the highly organized vortex ring wake produces the inflow of VRS.
