Reducing VRS to its simplest component requires a model that recognizes the primacy of vorticity in influencing the rotor. The simplest model that can do this is a “vortex ring emitter.” This model represents vertical flight by replacing the helical rotor wake with a sequence of individual vortex rings that are continually emitted, one for each blade, for each rotor revolution. The predictive capability of the model is fundamentally linked to its ability to not only capture sufficient revolutions of wake vorticity (up to 160/nb, where nb is the number of blades) but to allow the resulting wake elements to self-organize. Models that fail to retain sufficient wake vorticity, or that do not allow wake self-organization to take place, or that do not recognize the time-variant (vorticity accumulating) phase of a descent, will not be able to examine the full spectrum of VRS. Before developing the model, it is important to review a few fundamentals of vortex ring behavior. Vortex rings influence not only the surrounding flow but also themselves. As long as axial symmetry applies (as in pure vertical flight), each emitted ring will remain centered about the rotor’s vertical axis. Each vortex ring, by virtue of its own self-influence, will propel itself along the axial direction (usually downward and away from the rotor). Each ring is influenced by all the other rings that comprise the wake. Individual rings will contract or expand in diameter as they move relative to the other wake elements. The basic behavior can be observed in an experiment that is probably familiar to both chain smokers and aerodynamicists. It involves puffing two smoke rings in rapid succession, one behind the other. While the two smoke rings propel themselves away from the smoker, they also interact with each other. The trailing ring tends to be contracted (in its diameter) by the circulatory flow from the leading vortex ring. Conversely, the leading vortex ring experiences an outward flow due to the trailing ring’s influence. The vorticity of each ring is conserved; therefore, a diameter reduction will increase the selfinduced velocity of the trailing vortex, while the self-induced velocity of the leading vortex ring is reduced. The experiment shows that the trailing vortex will overtake the leading vortex, passing completely within its circle. After this, the pair, continuing to be propelled in the axial direction, repeats the leapfrogging process of swapping the leading and trailing position. In the simple wake model, emitted vortex rings become part of a Biot–Savart calculation that tracks the influence of each ring on itself and on the surrounding community of other rings. Axial symmetry is retained throughout the calculation, limiting the analysis to pure vertical flight (hover, climb, descent). Details governing the analysis of emitted vortex rings are provided in the Appendix. In the ring emitter model, rotor thrust is calculated using a simply articulated 11-segment blade element model that accounts for blade twist, collective, and the net inflow at each blade element to determine its effective angle of attack. Small angle assumptions are expressly avoided. Blade flapping response is permitted,
but in axially symmetric conditions its inclusion does not materially impact the solution. In axisymmetric conditions, flapping cannot alter the symmetry of the wake or change the fluid mechanism that allows vortex rings to accumulate and organize. For this model, emitted rings contain 100% of the blade-bound circulation, and there is no distributed sheet of vorticity. For simplicity, the emitted vortex ring strength is based on the average blade lift determined from the net rotor thrust. The core diameter was kept small at 0.075% of the rotor radius. To simulate freeflight conditions, the model trims to a hover condition and then allows the user to adjust the collective pitch. Any change in thrust from the initial trim condition will cause a climb (or a descent rate) to develop. A wind tunnel mode feature is also available for correlation with the body of existing VRS data. Wind tunnels are, however, fundamentally different from a free-flying rotor where VRS is concerned. This is because the wind tunnel flow (responsible for descent rate) does not respond to the rotor thrust. For a fixed collective pitch, wind tunnel models will experience large thrust loss (relative to hover trim thrust) as VRS is approached. Conversely, a free-flying rotorcraft will fall faster if thrust is reduced. This changes the rotor wake buildup by adding a free-stream component to the dynamics. Because of this interaction, most of the free-flying rotor’s descent will occur at close to 1g load factor conditions. Thrust loss, mitigated by downward acceleration, is how VRS is perceived in piloted rotorcraft. This free-body interaction does not take place in conventional wind tunnel experiments on VRS. Using the ring emitter model, the free-flight vertical descent process is taken to fully developed VRS in the sequence identified in Fig. 9. At the top of the figure, the wake patterns for the emitted rings are shown as snapshots at key phases of the descent. The time history data show the effect of the sequential reductions in rotor collective pitch of Fig. 9(a). From a stable hover at time = 0, a 0.25-deg collective drop at T = 25 s initiates a small descent rate. The initial thrust loss is seen in Fig. 9(b). However, at low descent rates, the thrust loss is fully and automatically recovered as a steady descent rate develops (black curve in Fig. 9(a)). Over the next four collective pitch drops to T = 325 or θ = 13◦, the main change in the observed wake pattern is that the tip vortex trajectory near the rotor tip (see corresponding insets in Fig. 9) transitions from an initial downward trajectory to an (initial) upward trajectory due to the free-stream influence. This lingering of the tip vortices above the rotor plane steadily increases the wake influence on the rotor as seen by the rise in vi/vih in Fig. 9(c) (recall that vi represents wake influence at the rotor disk). Nevertheless, up to this stage of descent, the net inflow through the rotor (Vz + vi) always returns to unity due to the beneficial effect (upflow) of the newly developed descent rate (see Fig. 9(c)). This is consistent with the positively damped region of Fig. 7 and indicates the full and complete restoration of 1g thrust in the rotor. A secondary effect of the new initial upward tip vortex trajectory is that it introduces a “rumble” into the rotor thrust (Fig. 9(b)). The “rumble” is due to the new tip vortices passing through the rotor plane (from above) as they propel themselves downward into the wake column. The resulting blade–vortex interactions are undoubtedly the cause of the unique acoustic signature reported by pilots who have approached the VRS condition at low forward speed. Two additional collective drops (at T = 400 and 475 s) further increase the descent rate to Vz/vih = −0.7. At this time, the pattern of ring vortices (corresponding to a collective pitch of θ = 12.3◦) is a considerably thicker column than previous patterns. Groupings of ring vortices that had appeared fur