the rotor as rapidly as new vorticity is added by the rotor blades. The equilibrium of net wake vorticity allows inflow to stabilize and thrust to recover to 1g at the new, higher descent rate, consistent with positive thrust damping. At T = 550 s, the collective drop (to θ = 12◦) initiates the most significant phase of VRS (i.e., the phase with negative thrust damping with respect to descent rate). All prior collective pitch drops settled to a higher, but constant, descent rate. Now, with no further collective pitch reductions, the rotor marches toward fully developed VRS and four wake pattern pictures are needed to show the dynamic transition that takes place in comparatively few seconds. In essence, the wake pattern shifts from an open loop (where vortices escape the vicinity of the rotor) to a closed loop, where wake vortices accumulate in an organized recirculating pattern. The entire lower portion of the wake column ejects vortices outward and upward, returning them to the rotor. The closed return path developing at θ = 12◦ represents the primary dynamic of VRS buildup, marked by a rapid growth in rotor inflow that is caused by a growing mass of highly organized vorticity. While each blade passage adds another vortex to the rapidly developing ring, the rotor is placed in a losing battle where inflow robs the blades of angle of attack and, therefore, lift. Once the rotor approaches the negatively damped region of Fig. 7, the thrust loss is so rapid that the rotor essentially falls through the ring, emerging with the ring above the rotor in a new, stable (positively damped) flow state (yet still under the influence of the vortex ring formation). As the entire wake structure collapses into a toroidal formation, the rotor inflow rises sharply as seen in Fig. 9(c) near T = 606 s. To appreciate the impact of this developing vortex ring, it is worthwhile to consider that the hovering rotor distributed its wake vorticity over an extensive helical column (Fig. 2) to generate the rotor’s hover-induced velocity field. In VRS, these same filaments are no longer distributed far below the rotor but are collapsed into a geometrically stable, highly organized ring structure in close proximity to the rotor. This highly ordered state multiplies the ability of the vortex filaments to produce inflow well beyond the hover value achieved by the distributed wake column. This is the reason that the inflow inferred from wind tunnel test data in Fig. 7 shows a 50% increase in net inflow compared to the hover value and surmises a tripling of the rotor wake strength (induced velocity) for fully developed VRS. The presence of a high inflow state (caused by the accumulating vortex ring) forces the rotor blades to low angles of attack and thrust loss is the integrated effect. This transition phase of VRS correlates with the stability boundary identified by Johnson (Ref. 6). It is also related closely to the Newman boundary defined using the wake transport velocity criteria in Ref. 9. In experimental flight testing, the entry into the negative damping phase corresponds to the descent rate where the normal load factor suddenly drops and is similarly related to the roll-off boundary identified in tiltrotors. On the basis of Fig. 9, the negatively damped region of Fig. 7 can never be considered a flight regime where steady conditions could be achieved because the ring and rotor are exchanging positions. The relationship of thrust to descent rate is negatively damped because in this phase, the ring is not only in an accumulating condition that increases inflow but is also in relative motion toward and through the rotor plane, where its proximity has a greater influence on the rotor. The closer the rotor approaches the ring, the more the rotor’s inflow increases, the more it loses thrust, and the more rapidly it descends toward (and through) the ring. This portion of VRS produces the “fall through” sensation reported by pilots and is in line with the observation (in Fig. 9 at T = 606 s) that thrust (transiently) drops to 60% of 1g before ending in a stable flight condition with the ring above the rotor. The transition (from a vortex ring below the rotor to a ring above the rotor) should not be confused with the transition between the climb
