ring formation has transitioned above the rotor, high stability (thrust damping with descent rate) is achieved. Thrust damping (with descent rate) also exists even if the vortex ring grows in strength (accumulates vorticity) transiently. An increase in strength will cause the ring to propel itself downward toward the rotor. The accumulated ring would overtake the rotor, except that the same ring causes the rotor to experience higher inflow. As the ring approaches the rotor, its influence on the rotor increases, producing the attendant reduction in blade effective angle of attack and thrust loss. With any thrust loss, the rotor accelerates
downward, increasing the gap between the ring and the rotor and allowing thrust to recover. In this way, a stable feedback mechanism exists to maintain the relative position of the ring and rotor after transition occurs. This discussion (relating to Fig. 11) is in contrast to steady descent wind tunnel test data such as Ref. 13, and more recently by Stack et al. (Ref. 14). It will be shown that the thrust-modulating feedback mechanism (present for free flight) does not exist in fixed-speed wind tunnel tests, and therefore the effects of VRS, in terms of thrust changes and the unsteady flow patterns, will tend to be overstated in such tests. The unsteady nature of VRS is often cited in background literature. More than 50 years ago, the flow visualization experiments of Drees and Hendal (Ref. 13) set the general impression of VRS as a rotor ingesting, scattering, and reingesting a chaotic wake formation. Yet, this impression is misleading where it concerns the relation to flight test data of rotorcraft. VRS requires the formation of a stable, highly organized vortex structure composed of accumulating wake elements. If this were not the case, a high inflow could not be achieved. As discussed next, the primary unsteadiness perceived in wind tunnel and water tunnel experiments of VRS is caused by a stable structure (the vortex ring) undergoing significant and repeated relative motion with respect to the rotor. In such tests, the chaotic impression is a fallout from testing the rotor at fixed descent rates that cannot represent a free-body rotorcraft. While the (fixed) tunnel speed sets the rotor’s descent rate, the ring’s descent rate (and its position relative to the rotor) remains ungoverned because the feedback mechanism controlling ring–rotor separation (in a free descent) is eliminated. When the descent rate is held fixed in the negatively damped region, the vortex ring will accumulate vorticity to the point that its self-induced propulsive speed exceeds the rotor’s (prescribed) descent rate. The ring propels itself downward (through and away from the rotor). The rotor inflow (caused by the ring) is negated, thrust recovers, and the free stream establishes a new wake pattern above the rotor. Quickly, a fresh vortex ring pattern emerges above the rotor, inflow builds, thrust is reduced, and the accumulating ring begins another cycle of passing downward through the rotor plane. Each time the ring approaches the rotor, thrust is reduced, but the rotor cannot accelerate (fall) away. Even though it experiences thrust loss, its “descent speed” is held fixed by the wind tunnel operation. The vortex ring will be able to propel itself downward directly through the rotor plane, dispersing vorticity below and repeating the process, as seen in flow visualization tests. With each cycle, a fresh vortex ring regenerates above the rotor and a new surge of inflow and thrust (loss) is created in a scenario that does not accurately represent the dynamics of free flight. Sustained thrust fluctuations on the order of the mean thrust level have been reported in such (fixed descent rate) tests. To examine the wind tunnel mode experiment, the vortex ring emitter model is started in a windmilling state (high descent rate) with collective pitch held constant. Figure 12 shows an initial vertical descent rate of approximately two times the hover-induced velocity followed by sequential reductions in the (wind tunnel) descent speed. With the reductions in descent rate, the rotor thrust decreases because the blades see a reduction in effective angle of attack due to the reduced (upward) free-stream flow. As the descent rate is further reduced between time = 30 s and time = 70 s, a vortex ring begins to gather above the rotor. Because of the fixed number of rings used in the emitter model (80), the accumulated vortex ring eventually cannot become any stronger (old rings disappear from the calculation of inflow as rapidly as new rings are created). For this descent rate, the accumulated ring remains in a fixed position above the rotor. Reductions in descent rate can be continued, allowing the organizing ring to drive downward through the rotor plane. Alternatively, realizing that the 80-ring limitation (used in the emitter model) is arbitrary, more rings can be permitted in the calculation. In this case, the number of emitted
