Fighting VRS: Example of Increasing Collective Pitch
In a descent toward VRS, gradually adding collective pitch to the rotor does not stop the addition of tip vortices to the ring. Therefore, a straightforward “add more thrust” approach to tackling VRS is not likely to succeed. A higher collective pitch simply allows the rotor to equalize an even stronger vortex ring (and achieve higher descent rates) before transition occurs. To examine the “free-flight” behavior of increasing collective pitch during VRS, the ring emitter model is started in a windmilling condition and the collective pitch is increased incrementally, as seen in Fig. 14. In Fig. 14, the free-flight rotor starts in a windmill state (at T = 0) with a descent speed that is roughly twice the hover-induced velocity. To arrest the descent rate, the collective pitch is gradually increased as shown in Fig. 14(a). As seen in Fig. 14(c), the thrust initially increases and then trends toward the 1g hover value. While the wake pattern remains columnar (and above the rotor), the descent speed decreases smoothly with collective pitch as one would expect in the positively damped regime. However, after T = 33 s (collective pitch ∼13 deg), further increases in collective pitch cease to arrest the descent rate. This point marks the formation of an accumulating ring vortex above the rotor disk. The growing induced flow of the vortex ring suppresses any increase in effective blade pitch angle (due to collective pitch increases). Any improvement in moderating the descent rate from the next 7 deg of collective pitch increase (from T = 33 s to T = 85 s) is completely canceled by the presence of the ring’s inflow. In this time period, moderate thrust fluctuations (±20% of the mean thrust) are observed, yet the vortex ring remains above the rotor. At 20 deg of collective pitch, the rotor finally climbs through the vortex ring, ultimately achieving a vertical climb rate that well exceeds the hover inflow velocity. The inability to arrest the descent rate over the majority of Fig. 14 is consistent with pilot reports of the collective pitch being ineffective during VRS. The only inconsistent result is that the simulation is eventually able to “climb out” of VRS. Note, however, that the collective pitch range available in the model may not represent normal helicopter
