Maneuvering in VRS
In all cases, the strategy for exiting VRS is to create distance between the established ring and the rotor. In Ref. 2, Kisor et al. reported that maneuvering in the V-22 suppressed the appearance of VRS. The suppression of VRS in accelerated flight has since been reported in the analytical work of Ahlin and Brown (Ref. 15). An explanation of this behavior is made here on the basis of considering the ring and rotor as separate entities. A vortex ring can propel itself only in a single axial direction. To participate in a maneuver, new rings (that are oriented to the new direction of flight) must be formed. The old established ring will continue on the original trajectory, separating itself (and its influence) from the rotor. This offers an insight into why the V-22 test team could penetrate deeply into the quasi-steady VRS boundary with no signs of VRS during dynamic maneuvers. If a rotor can displace itself from the ring or accelerate in a direction that the established ring cannot follow, the rotor will tend toward VRS-free conditions. In part, this also explains why VRS cannot extend to higher forward speeds. In forward flight, the ring can stay with the rotor only if the ring’s axis is effectively tilted so that its self-propulsion has a forward speed component. Alternatively, the leading edge of the ring must be replenished with vorticity as fast as the trailing edge is depleted. In a sustained “edgewise” acceleration, new tip vortices would have to continuously rebuild portions of the “old” established ring that did not follow the rotor. Clearly, it is much more difficult to intensify a vortex ring in an accelerated condition. The greater the acceleration (due to a turn), the deeper the rotor can penetrate into the boundary of descent rates and low speeds that would produce VRS in steady flight conditions. A sudden reorientation of the rotor tip path plane effectively establishes a new thrust vector and introduces an acceleration that does not support ring accumulation. Acceleration drives the rotor away from any ring formation that was formed in the quasi-steady preaccelerated descent. This is likely the reason why the action of nacelle tilt proved to be such a powerful mechanism for recovering from the VRS condition during V-22 flight trials.
The Future of VRS Research
Most wind tunnel tests of VRS have placed a model rotor at fixed collective pitch into a steady free stream. Steady-state wind/water tunnel tests show significant mean thrust variations with the descent rate and sustained thrust fluctuations as large as 80% of the mean thrust in steady descents. When the model rotor encounters VRS, thrust loss is measured, but the free-stream speed that is creating the descent rate remains unchanged and the rotor continues to descend at the same rate with a substantially reduced thrust. Flight test results and the ring emitter simulations show that this static behavior is not representative of an actual rotorcraft in VRS flight conditions. For a free-flying rotor, increased sink rate must accompany thrust loss, which completely changes the relationship between the free stream, the ring, and the rotor. Flight test results for the V22 (Ref. 2) showed that VRS is a relatively high-thrust phenomenon where the mean thrust rarely dropped below 90% of 1g normal load factor. Results also showed a smooth progression into fully developed VRS with none of the repeated thrust-surging behavior seen in wind tunnel tests. Although wind tunnel testing with a free-stream speed that responds to thrust loss would be a challenge, such experimental results would provide
a more accurate assessment of the growth, development, and behavior of VRS in free flight. A dynamic test rig may be able to facilitate such testing and could lead to a more rigorous experimental and theoretical basis for suppressing VRS. Results may offer added details on why maneuvers, control inputs, and the action of nacelle tilting (in the V-22) are such powerful mechanisms for extracting the aircraft from fully developed VRS. The power of flow visualization in examining rotor flow states should not be overlooked. Flow patterns can reveal an exceptional amount of detail concerning flow content. The flow visualization experiments of Drees and Hendal (Ref. 13) were completed more than 55 years ago, and this work has been cited in almost every publication concerning VRS research. It would be a missed opportunity to omit flow visualization from a new experimental test program.
Conclusions
1) The fundamental dynamic of VRS is the propensity for vortex wake elements to accumulate in a highly organized structure that multiplies the induced flow experienced by the descending rotor. Rotor inflow increases with VRS, causing the blades to operate at a reduced effective angle of attack and lose thrust. While wake filament disorganization can occur as the wake ages, it is not the characterizing feature that enables VRS. The organized part of the wake is responsible for increasing the rotor inflow, and inflow will reduce the effective blade angle of attack. The organized accumulation of the wake (in a low forward air speed descent) is therefore the characterizing feature of VRS. 2) Steady speed wind tunnel tests do not appropriately simulate the primary, unsteady event of VRS (where the ring transitions through the rotor plane a single time). Steady conditions can exist prior to, and after, transition—if the accumulated vortex ring is maintaining its position relative to the rotor. However, even in these steady conditions, wind tunnel results will typically show that the thrust is below that required for sustained 1g flight. In free flight, thrust loss is mitigated by higher descent rates that are precluded by a fixed wind tunnel speed. For a rotorcraft in a free descent, VRS remains a relatively high thrust condition until the ring transitions through the rotor plane. 3) The transition of the (vortex) ring from a position below the rotor to a position above the rotor is the “main event” in VRS. It is associated with a thrust surge that happens once in a free descent but repeats continuously in “steady speed” wind tunnel testing. The unsteadiness perceived in wind tunnel tests comes primarily from a stable structure (the vortex ring) undergoing repeated relative motion through the rotor disk. The simple ring emitter model clearly predicts the VRS initiation process and traces the origin of the negatively (thrust) damped region to an accumulating vortex ring that undergoes relative motion through the rotor. The ring’s accumulation and its eventual transit through the rotor disk causes the blades to operate at progressively lower effective angles of attack and lose thrust. 4) The stable relationship between thrust and descent rate/inflow on either side of the negatively damped region (of Fig. 7) has been related to stable wake formations where organized vorticity is added to the vortex ring as quickly as it is depleted/dispersed/disorganized. When the toroidal wake is not accumulating organized vorticity, the descent rate relationship with rotor thrust is positively damped. 5) VRS is a perturbation from the upper (working-state) branch of simple momentum theory’s inflow state curves. The transition of the vortex ring through the rotor is not associated with the transition to the windmilling branch. 6) The accumulated vortex ring takes time to develop and once established can only propel itself in a single axial direction. The rotor can maneuver, but the ring cannot. Therefore, maneuvers suppress the effects of VRS.
